    <h2>The Bug You Don't See Is the One That Matters</h2>

    <p>Most pest calls in the Shoals are about visible nuisances — ants, roaches, the occasional wasp nest, mice moving in when it cools off. Those are real, and worth handling. The pest that actually decides a house's fate is the one nobody sees: the subterranean termite, working from the soil up into the framing, hollowing out wood from the inside while the surface still looks solid. By the time a homeowner notices mud tubes on a foundation wall or a wing pile by a windowsill after a spring swarm, the colony has often been feeding for a long time. The whole reason termites are a big deal here is that the damage is invisible until it's expensive.</p>

    <h2>Why One-and-Done Never Holds</h2>

    <p>The tempting move is a single treatment — spray the perimeter, knock down what's visible, and skip the recurring plan to save the fee. In this climate that reliably fails. A one-time barrier breaks down over months, the next generation of pests moves back into the same conducive conditions that drew the first, and termites never announce that the barrier has lapsed. The failure pattern is a homeowner who cancels the quarterly service after a quiet year, then discovers a termite problem two years later that the lapsed bond would have caught at an inspection. Pest pressure here is continuous, so protection has to be continuous — the seasons don't take a year off.</p>

    <h2>Humidity Is the Silent Partner</h2>

    <p>Here's the counterintuitive part: the most effective pest control on an older Shoals home is often about water, not chemicals. Termites, roaches, and wood-destroying fungus all follow moisture, and the region's humid climate keeps crawlspaces damp unless someone manages them. A practitioner who knows this market checks the crawlspace first — standing water, poor drainage, wood in contact with soil, a torn vapor barrier — because fixing those conducive conditions does more to keep termites out than any single spray. On the wood-frame historic stock in the Florence and Sheffield districts, a dry, ventilated crawlspace and a maintained barrier is the real protection. The honest admission the industry avoids: no treatment is permanent, because the pressure never stops, which is exactly why the maintained relationship is the product.</p>

    <h2>What Pest Control Runs in the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Recurring general service</strong> — about $40 to $70 per quarter, with a first visit often $150 to $250.</li>
      <li><strong>Termite treatment</strong> — roughly $1,200 to $3,000 for a liquid barrier or bait-station system, driven by home size and foundation type.</li>
      <li><strong>Annual termite renewal or bond</strong> — usually $150 to $350, the cheapest structural insurance a homeowner buys.</li>
      <li><strong>Repair of real termite damage</strong> — frequently five figures, which is the number the whole plan exists to avoid.</li>
    </ul>

    <p id="pricing-summary">Rural and wooded lots out toward the Colbert County satellites — Cherokee, Leighton, and Littleville — often carry heavier pressure from the tree line, which can nudge the recurring interval tighter.</p>

    <h2>The Signs That Mean Call Now, Not Later</h2>

    <p>Mud tubes running up a foundation wall or pier are termites building a covered highway to your wood, and that's a same-week inspection, not a wait-and-see. A pile of discarded wings on a windowsill after a warm spring day means a swarm just happened inside or against the house. And a floor that's gone soft underfoot, or trim that crumbles when you press it, means the damage is already structural and the colony has been there a while — that's a call today.</p>

